LOGBOOK.md:250:- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` is now **prohibited on all four macOS targets**. Rule **K2**: granted only where a **named second target** in the contract reads the same Data Protection Keychain item. Sharing is the entitlement's *only* function — it does not unlock the DPK, change durability, or survive anything the default group does not — so any argument for it that cannot name a second reader is an argument for something it does not do. On macOS there is no second reader: K1 excludes the root provider, and neither probe touches the DPK.
LOGBOOK.md:252:- FINDING (**the trap**): the entitlement array is ordered `keychain-access-groups` **first**, then the app identifier — the first element is the default when a caller names none. So withdrawing the entitlement does not only remove sharing, it **moves the default group**: `262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared` → `262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac`. An unqualified macOS write would have landed somewhere different before and after. Nothing is written yet so there is no migration, but `TASK-260715-379cpk` must not pass `kSecAttrAccessGroup` on macOS (it now returns `errSecMissingEntitlement`) while iOS still names the shared group explicitly — a platform branch, not a shared constant. **The verdict did not name this; it fell out of reading the source the verdict cited.**
LOGBOOK.md:254:- DECISION: `com.apple.security.application-groups` is now **prohibited on the iOS probe pair** (verdict 04 F3). r4 granted it because the probe "exercises the same shared-container path production depends on" — but `TASK-260715-1jckn0` scopes the probe to the `NETunnelProviderManager` lifecycle and versioned app messaging, its AC3 puts the whole exchange on `sendProviderMessage`, and no shared container appears anywhere. Rule G4 gained **`probeRule`**: a probe inherits **identifiers and profile classes, never entitlements**; if a probe is meant to prove a capability can be *provisioned*, that is a verification deliverable and must be an acceptance criterion **first** — the entitlement follows the criterion, not the reverse.
